<BaseLayout
  title="5 Signs Your Water Heater May Need Replacement"
  description="Learn the common warning signs that a water heater is nearing the end of its useful life and when repair may no longer be the best option."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a> <span>/</span>
        <a href="/blog/">Blog</a> <span>/</span>
        <span>Replacement Signs</span>
      </nav>
      <h1>5 Signs Your Water Heater May Need Replacement</h1>
      <p>
        <time datetime="2025-09-12">September 12, 2025</time>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          Water heaters work quietly in the background until something goes wrong. Knowing when
          repair still makes sense—and when replacement is the smarter move—can save money and
          prevent unexpected cold showers.
        </p>

        <h2>1. The Unit Is More Than 10–12 Years Old</h2>
        <p>
          Most traditional tank water heaters last roughly 8 to 12 years. Once a unit enters that
          range, the likelihood of additional failures increases. If you are already facing a
          significant repair on an older tank, comparing the repair cost to a new installation is
          worthwhile.
        </p>

        <h2>2. Rust-Colored Water or Visible Corrosion</h2>
        <p>
          Discolored hot water can indicate internal tank corrosion. Once the steel tank begins to
          rust through, patching is rarely a lasting solution. Replacement becomes the practical path.
        </p>

        <h2>3. Persistent Leaks Around the Tank</h2>
        <p>
          A drip from a fitting or valve can often be fixed. Water pooling at the base of the tank
          itself usually means the tank has failed. Continuing to use a leaking tank risks water
          damage to floors and nearby materials.
        </p>

        <h2>4. Rising Energy Bills Without Other Explanation</h2>
        <p>
          Sediment buildup and declining efficiency can cause a water heater to use more energy for
          the same amount of hot water. If bills have climbed and the unit is aging, a newer,
          better-insulated model may reduce operating costs.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Frequent Repairs</h2>
        <p>
          One repair is normal. Multiple service calls in a short period often signal that the unit
          is wearing out. At that point, the cumulative cost of repairs can approach or exceed the
          cost of replacement.
        </p>

        <h2>What to Do Next</h2>
        <p>
          If several of these signs apply to your water heater, schedule an inspection. A technician
          can assess the condition of the tank, anode rod, and other components and help you weigh
          repair versus replacement based on your specific situation.
        </p>
        <p>
          For service in Mooresville and the Lake Norman area,
          <a href="/contact/">contact us</a> or call for an appointment.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
